\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Common-$H$ synthetic recovery. Means and marginal bootstrap intervals use six clean structural templates after averaging three mask seeds and two noise settings. The target includes the prescribed noise. Left: fixed curvelet recovery. Right: a frozen four-candidate recovery pool with observed-only validation and gain selection. No comparison survives the 24-test template-level Holm family.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:recovery}{{3}{7}{Common-$H$ synthetic recovery. Means and marginal bootstrap intervals use six clean structural templates after averaging three mask seeds and two noise settings. The target includes the prescribed noise. Left: fixed curvelet recovery. Right: a frozen four-candidate recovery pool with observed-only validation and gain selection. No comparison survives the 24-test template-level Holm family}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces One real source under the unconfirmed IEEE-LE payload interpretation. Points average two common geometric splits; bars show their observed minimum--maximum range, not confidence intervals. Window and temporal-resampling sensitivities are all retained. The horizontal zero line is the zero-prediction SNR. Different windows have different targets and cannot be ranked as same-task gains.}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:real}{{4}{7}{One real source under the unconfirmed IEEE-LE payload interpretation. Points average two common geometric splits; bars show their observed minimum--maximum range, not confidence intervals. Window and temporal-resampling sensitivities are all retained. The horizontal zero line is the zero-prediction SNR. Different windows have different targets and cannot be ranked as same-task gains}{figure.4}{}}
